course: french
lesson: 36
title: English words that came from French
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: You already know thousands of French words without knowing it: menu, restaurant, café, ballet, garage and souvenir all came into English from French.
explain: The big wave came after 1066, when the Normans from France ruled England, and for about three hundred years French was the language of the king's court, the law and the rich.
explain: Picture a farm in 1100: the English-speaking farmer looks after a cow, a pig and a sheep, but when the meat reaches the French-speaking lord's table it is called beef, pork and mutton - from French bœuf, porc and mouton.
explain: Some borrowed words are hiding: a pilot shouting "Mayday!" is copying French m'aider, "help me", and RSVP on a party invitation stands for "Répondez, s'il vous plaît".
explain: Borrowed words can change on the way: souvenir means any memory in French, chauffeur means any driver, and French audiences shout "Bis !" where English audiences shout "Encore!"
fact: Thousands of English words come from French; after 1066 the Normans from France ruled England and French became the language of the court.
fact: Menu, restaurant and café are French words used in English.
fact: Beef comes from bœuf, pork from porc, mutton from mouton and veal (calf meat) from veau: the farmers kept English names for the animals, and the rich French speakers named the meat.
fact: Voilà means "there you go" or "there it is"; it comes from vois là, "see there", and is spelled with an accent, not "walla".
fact: Déjà vu means "already seen": the feeling that something has happened before; déjà means already, and its j is a soft "zh" sound, like the s in treasure.
fact: The emergency call Mayday comes from French m'aider, "help me"; it has nothing to do with the month of May.
fact: RSVP stands for Répondez, s'il vous plaît, "Please reply".
fact: English audiences shout "Encore!" for more; French audiences usually shout "Bis !"
fact: Un souvenir is a keepsake, but in French it also means any memory; se souvenir means to remember.
fact: Bon voyage ! means "Have a good trip!" and can be said to anyone setting off anywhere.
fact: Ballet grew up at the French court, so its steps have French names such as plié and pirouette, and the t of ballet is silent.
fact: Chauffeur means any driver in French; it comes from chauffer, to heat, because the first drivers of steam engines kept the fire hot.
fact: Garage is a French word: French says "ga-RAZH"; British English often says "GARR-idge" and American English "guh-RAHZH", and both are fine in English.
exercise: Jayden says menu, restaurant and café are English words. Where did they come from? | From French | From Latin directly | From German | From Italian
exercise: When did lots of French words come into English? | After 1066, when the Normans ruled England | After 1966, when England won the World Cup | Before the Romans ever came to Britain | Only in the last fifty years, from TV
exercise: Which English meat word comes from French bœuf? | beef | bull | buffalo | bacon
exercise: Why are cow and pig English, but beef and pork French? | Farmers spoke English; the rich who ate the meat spoke French | French people had never seen a cow or a pig | English people did not eat meat until 1500 | The words were chosen at random by a king
exercise: How do you spell the word Ava texts as "walla"? | voilà | voila | wala | vwala
exercise: What does voilà come from? | vois là, "see there" | voir l'eau, "see water" | voile, "a sail" | vol, "a flight"
exercise: Lily says "déjà vu" when her brother loses his shoes again. What does déjà mean? | Already | Again | Never | Today
exercise: Where does "Mayday!" come from? | French m'aider, "help me" | The month of May | May Day, the spring holiday | An English sailor called May
exercise: What does RSVP stand for? | Répondez, s'il vous plaît | Reply soon, very please | Rendez-vous, s'il vous plaît | Restez sur votre place
exercise: What do French audiences usually shout for more? | Bis ! | Encore ! | Bravo ! | Merci !
exercise: Isla buys a souvenir in Paris. What else does souvenir mean in French? | A memory | A fridge magnet only | A postcard | A present for a teacher
exercise: Oscar's nan is going to Spain. Can he say "Bon voyage !"? | Yes, it's for any trip | No, only for trips to France | No, only for sea trips | Yes, but only in French lessons
exercise: Why are ballet steps like plié and pirouette French? | Ballet grew up at the French court | Ballet was invented in England | French is the only language for dance | A French teacher named them in 1990
exercise: How do you say ballet? | "ba-LAY", with a silent t | "BAL-ett", with a t | "ba-LETT", with a t | "BALL-ay", like a football
exercise: In French, who is a chauffeur? | Any driver, even a bus driver | Only a posh driver in a uniform | Only someone who drives a king | A person who heats your food
exercise: Where does chauffeur come from? | chauffer, to heat | chaussure, a shoe | chauve, bald | chef, a cook
exercise: How is garage said in French? | "ga-RAZH" | "GARR-idge" | "GAR-ahj" | "ga-RAYJ"
test: Your mate goes, "French and English have nothing in common, bruv." What do you say? | Thousands of English words came from French | He's right, they share no words at all | Only the word "bonjour" is shared | English came from French completely
test: Liam from Dublin goes, "Sure the cow stayed English and the dinner went French?" Is he right? | Yes: the animal kept its English name, the meat got a French one | No: both words are English | No: both words are French | Yes: but only in Ireland
test: Ava from Ohio texts "walla!" after finishing her art. What should she write? | voilà, with an accent on the à | walla, it's fine as it is | voila, with no accent at all | wallah, with an h at the end
test: ★ Charlie from Leeds says, "Mayday is about May Day, the spring holiday, like." What's true? | It comes from French m'aider, "help me" | He's right, it's about the spring | It comes from an English king called May | It means "today" in old English
test: Mia from Melbourne sees RSVP on a party invite and goes, "What even is that, heaps confusing." What should she do? | Reply to say if she's coming | Bring a present for the host | Wear fancy dress | Arrive very early
test: ★ Ben from Boston shouts "Encore!" at a concert in Paris. What would French people more often shout? | Bis ! | Au revoir ! | Bon voyage ! | Voilà !
test: Isla from Edinburgh writes "J'ai de bons souvenirs de Paris." Her pal thinks it means she bought fridge magnets. What does it mean? | I have good memories of Paris | I bought lots of keepsakes in Paris | I have good friends in Paris | I have nice postcards from Paris
test: ★★★ A French bus driver, a taxi driver and a posh limousine driver - which of them can be called chauffeur in French? | All three | Only the limousine driver | Only the taxi driver | None of them
test: Kai from Auckland says, "Chur, so all ballet words are French? Plié, pirouette..." Why? | Ballet grew up at the French court | Ballet was invented in New Zealand | They were just chosen because they sound nice | French is the language of all sports
test: Harry from Kent says "GARR-idge" and his US cousin says "guh-RAHZH". Who's right? | Both are fine in English; French says "ga-RAZH" | Only Harry, the British way | Only the cousin, the American way | Neither, it's "GAR-age" with a hard g
test: What would happen if you said "déjà vu" in English with a hard English j, "day-ja voo"? | People would understand, but the French says a soft zh | It would mean "never seen" | It would sound like a rude word | Nobody would understand at all
test: ★★★ Beef, pork and mutton come from bœuf, porc and mouton. Veal comes from French veau. Which English animal word did the farmers keep for it? | calf | cow | lamb | goat
exam: Identify the historical event which occasioned the entry of a great number of French words into English. | The rule of England by the Normans after 1066 | The arrival of the Romans in 43 AD | The invention of printing in 1440 | The opening of the Channel Tunnel in 1994
exam: Ascertain the French expression from which the distress call "Mayday" is derived. | m'aider | mai | médaille | maison
exam: State in full the French expression abbreviated as RSVP. | Répondez, s'il vous plaît | Rendez-vous, s'il vous plaît | Restez sur votre place | Revenez s'il vous plaît
exam: Determine the meaning of "chauffeur" in contemporary French. | Any driver of a vehicle | Exclusively a driver of private cars for the wealthy | A person employed to heat a building | A person who cooks in a restaurant
